<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · concept</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lingam</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as linga, Shiva linga</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A lingam, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> for sign or mark, is an abstract or aniconic representation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaivism">Shaivism</a>, usually a short cylindrical pillar of stone, metal, wood, clay or precious stone. It often stands within a disc-shaped platform, the yoni, its feminine counterpart, shaped to let liquid offerings drain away. Though rooted in representations of the male organ, it is regarded in Shaivism as the outward symbol of formless reality, and with the yoni as the union of the feminine and masculine that recreates all existence. It is typically the main devotional image in temples of Shiva; the Gudimallam Lingam, one of the oldest, is mostly dated to the 3rd to 1st century BCE.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lingam.jpg" alt="Lingam" width="330" height="495" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: picasaweb.google.com · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mukhalinga.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
